WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.982 --> 00:00:08.440
ANDY NICHOLLS: For
those of you who

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:11.440
don't work in the pharmaceutical
industry, what this--

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.060
and indeed, for
those of you who do,

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:18.430
this diagram in front of you
is essentially an org chart

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:19.750
and it's also a timeline.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:22.270
So this is how the
biostatistics department at GSK

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:23.440
is structured.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.540
I say it's like a timeline
because our structure

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:28.043
essentially reflects the
drug development lifecycle.

00:00:28.043 --> 00:00:30.710
So this can take up to 15 years,
which is why this at the bottom

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:32.702
is presenting.

00:00:32.702 --> 00:00:34.660
And that takes us to--
so the research in vivo,

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:36.700
in vitro true
analysis all the way

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:40.250
through to the
clinical development.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:44.530
And by and large, if you're
not from industry, or you are

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.050
and that's familiar to
you, you can slice this up

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:50.260
into two distinct ways
of working, really.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.170
The first is the sort
of non-clinical area.

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:56.630
And that's actually where
my group [INAUDIBLE] is set.

00:00:56.630 --> 00:01:00.580
This is where research
statistics on our manufacturing

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.128
statistics sit as well.

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:04.420
And that's a large group of
statisticians, programmers,

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:07.060
data scientists--
right about 100 people.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:09.940
But overwhelmingly, the vast
majority of people in our group

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:11.560
sit within the clinical space.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.653
And the clinical space has
very, very different controls

00:01:14.653 --> 00:01:16.570
and ways of working to
the non-clinical space.

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:18.403
And it's the clinical
space that we're going

00:01:18.403 --> 00:01:19.730
to be talking about today.

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:26.800
So a team of 500 clinical "data
scientists," we can call them.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:31.233
And I put "data scientists"
in quotes on these slides.

00:01:31.233 --> 00:01:33.650
Because really, if you speak
to anyone in that department,

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:35.567
very few people would
actually call themselves

00:01:35.567 --> 00:01:36.860
a data scientist.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:38.360
They are made up
of people whose job

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:41.330
title is either statistician
or programmer generally.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:44.480
And one of the reasons why
I think that I would also

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.180
probably not call them
data scientists in a sense

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:50.000
is that for me, a
data scientist is

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.370
someone who is multi-skilled
in a number of different ways.

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:55.940
So they're not simply
a statistician.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:57.020
They can code a bit as well.

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:58.395
They can use
different languages.

00:01:58.395 --> 00:01:59.960
They use the right
tool for the job.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.835
And although there's a
huge amount of talent,

00:02:01.835 --> 00:02:05.120
that 500 statisticians
or programmers,

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.400
at the start of
our journey, they

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:10.160
weren't really data
scientists in the sense

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:11.990
that I'm describing.

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:14.780
I'm just to give you an
idea of what kinds of skills

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:17.630
we do have, away from just pure
statistics and pure programming

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:18.350
skill.

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:22.430
I put up some rough
estimates of proficiency

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:26.870
within department at the
start of this journey in 2018.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:32.930
And as you can see,
the overwhelming party

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:35.120
can program in
stats I've said 99%.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:37.790
I put 100%, but I'm allowing
myself a little bit of room

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:39.290
for one or two people who can't.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:42.332
But essentially everybody
can program in SAS

00:02:42.332 --> 00:02:44.540
There is some proficiency
in R in the clinical space,

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:47.600
but largely R has
been used historically

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.480
for the generation of
graphics, and SAS still

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.000
dominates in that area.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.858
It's normally bespoke.

00:02:53.858 --> 00:02:56.150
Graphics are particularly
difficult to generate in SAS.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:03:00.710
But R has been useful along
with a few very proactive R

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:02.460
users pushing off of graphics.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:05.960
And for clinical
trial simulations

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:12.860
where R arguably lends itself
better towards simulations.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:15.410
Python again, I'm trying to
cover myself with 1% percent.

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:20.060
Essentially, nobody's using
Python in this clinical space.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.800
But just in case, I put 1%.

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:23.850
And Julia, essentially,
I put up there

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:26.480
to make it sound like I
know what I'm talking about.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.810
Essentially, no one
is using [INAUDIBLE]..

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:33.830
So why are we not using R?

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:39.050
Well, a lot of it comes
down to the past 20 or 30

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:40.490
years of drug development.

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:45.970
And skills have been built
up over that time in SAS.

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:48.770
And alongside those
skills, the tools we use,

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:50.510
what are called SAS macros.

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:52.580
Which, if you're not
familiar with SAS,

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:55.070
would be akin to an R function.

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:57.260
You can think of all the
functions and packages

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.420
you might build in R.
All those kind of tools

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:01.760
that we build to make our
job easier are all have all

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:03.140
been built in SAS.

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:06.590
And in a clinical space, it's
very, very heavily regulated.

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:10.640
And so we have systems that
control to a very fine nature

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:12.900
our workflows that we use.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.650
And these systems as well
are entirely built for SAS.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:21.035
Add all that to the fact
that it's very conservative.

00:04:21.035 --> 00:04:22.910
The further along you
get along the pipeline,

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:27.590
the more conservative it
gets within our industry.

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:31.700
And you can start to see
why R hasn't been used.

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:33.680
But the real one--

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:36.050
the one I hear a lot
is this statement here.

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:38.592
The regulators only accepts
[INAUDIBLE] performed in SAS.

00:04:38.592 --> 00:04:40.550
This is essentially a
myth that has been around

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:42.060
for a long time.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.280
It gets repeated in
various different forms.

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:47.260
I'll now try to give
you an idea of the one

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:49.628
on the slide as to
what really that means

00:04:49.628 --> 00:04:51.170
and where that comes
from-- at least,

00:04:51.170 --> 00:04:52.843
where I think it comes from.

00:04:52.843 --> 00:04:54.260
So you have, for
example, when you

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:57.440
submit funding for submission
with the FDA, the Food and Drug

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:02.370
Administration America, you
have to provide data sets

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:03.860
and assess transport format.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:05.090
And that's an open format.

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:07.220
And this statement is--

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:10.460
I put a link in the bottom
as to where that comes from.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:12.732
This statement here
is from the FDA.

00:05:12.732 --> 00:05:13.940
[INAUDIBLE] an open standard.

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:16.070
I put in line a code,
submit a pseudocode

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:18.020
on the right-hand
side to highlight just

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:20.610
how easy that is to
generate in R. In fact,

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:24.020
it's less code in R hat it
would take in SAS ironically.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:28.010
But these kinds of things,
these kinds of statements

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:28.760
get put out there.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:31.700
And I think because it has
[INAUDIBLE] there's always

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:33.752
this assumption
SAS has to be used.

00:05:33.752 --> 00:05:35.960
What people on very, very
close to the submission end

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:38.177
of things, it's easy to
see how that gets changed

00:05:38.177 --> 00:05:39.260
and transformed over time.

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:44.690
And people get very used to
SAS-dominated way of working.

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:48.020
On the regulator note, I
could talk for an entire hour.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:53.440
I won't be, but I could talk for
an entire hour on this topic.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:55.520
Phil mentioned that
in the introduction.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.073
If you are interested in
the regulatory barriers

00:05:59.073 --> 00:06:00.740
or perceived regulatory
barriers that we

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:02.115
face in the
pharmaceutical space,

00:06:02.115 --> 00:06:06.380
I would recommend you
go to www.pharmar.org.

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:08.610
This is the all validation
hub that Phil mentioned.

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:12.050
And you can see
here in particular,

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:16.916
a white paper on risk-based
approach to package validation.

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:22.290
Given all these
barriers and challenges,

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:24.510
why are we looking at R now?

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:28.050
I think one of the key reasons
is the influx of new talent

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:31.860
and skill around R coming
from college, from university.

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:36.110
And we may have people
who have been using SAS

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:39.710
to 20 or 30 years,
but [INAUDIBLE] people

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:42.410
with skills in R,
generally, they're not

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:44.000
coming out with skills in SAS.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.610
And what we're doing is because
no one else understands R

00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:49.520
very well, we're
having to retrain them.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:52.040
All of their education
has been in R.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:55.310
And we're retraining them
in SAS to make them--

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:58.400
so they can work
with everybody else.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:01.190
And now, I think it's
probably fair to say

00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:02.360
we've reached critical mass.

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:03.570
And we've got so
many people who never

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:06.070
want to use R. We're now looking
at it the other way around.

00:07:06.070 --> 00:07:08.210
Let's retrain some of our--

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:12.710
some of the people who've been
there for 20 or 30 years in R.

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:15.120
The other piece is the
drive for innovation.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:15.920
And that's not new.

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:17.630
We're Always driving
for innovation.

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:19.670
But I think there's a
few things in particular

00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:21.700
I've highlighted on this slide.

00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:25.400
The last two-- the
communication of visualization

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:27.360
and the productivity
gains I mentioned.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:30.620
I'll go through a bit more
detail in the next slide.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:34.160
For now, just to talk about the
methodologies and simulation,

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:38.840
what we're seeing is
an increased drive

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:45.140
to use the latest statistical
methodologies to try and design

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:46.578
more efficient trials.

00:07:46.578 --> 00:07:48.620
And when I talk about more
efficient trials, what

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:52.323
I mean is if you think about
a drug getting to market,

00:07:52.323 --> 00:07:53.990
up until the point
where you have proved

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:56.750
the efficacy and the
safety of that drug,

00:07:56.750 --> 00:08:01.010
your drug is something that you
want to expose as few people

00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:03.320
to as possible.

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:05.480
It's a potentially
dangerous compound

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:07.070
that we want to
make sure that we

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:08.660
were minimizing that exposure.

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:10.390
So we need to reduce that.

00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:13.315
And equally, if it is
effective, and it's safe,

00:08:13.315 --> 00:08:15.440
we want to get it to market
as quickly as possible.

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:16.970
And there's been a
lot of investment

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:22.370
in things like [INAUDIBLE]
GSK and other methodologies

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:24.920
to try and create a
more streamlined process

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:27.060
for developing drugs.

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:30.500
And a lot of that requires
heavy amounts of stimulation.

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:31.940
And there are a
lot of tools that

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:33.799
are built around that
are available in R.

00:08:33.799 --> 00:08:37.015
So they're really, really
pushing things forward as well.

00:08:37.015 --> 00:08:38.390
Before we move
onto the last two,

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:39.932
I'm going to talk
about visualization

00:08:39.932 --> 00:08:42.052
and the dynamic
reporting aspects.

00:08:42.052 --> 00:08:43.760
I just want to mention
a couple of others

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:48.050
that often get talked about
in relation to adoption of R.

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:50.420
The first one
being cost, I think

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:55.940
cost is important [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:59.930
It wouldn't put it as
it as important as any

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:02.360
of the other things on
this slide actually.

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:06.110
When you think about cost, yes,
R is free and tools like SAS

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:06.740
are not.

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:09.950
But there's a huge cost
in training 500 people

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:15.800
and updating all of our
standard tools, and systems,

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:17.130
and so on as well.

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:19.500
So in a sense that
the cost, yes, there's

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:22.280
some long-term cost savings,
but in the short term,

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:25.980
it's probably the
other way around.

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:27.460
And then the last
one, the gateway

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:29.410
to Python and open source--

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:31.810
everything I'm talking about
here with respect to R,

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:34.870
you could switch out
R and put in Python,

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:37.480
or any other open source tool.

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:41.530
R is being more statistical,
I guess, in nature,

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:44.987
at least in its origins compared
to something like Python.

00:09:44.987 --> 00:09:47.320
It's a more natural language
in the pharmaceutical space

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:49.780
to use.

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:52.270
And that's why we've
chosen it as the tool

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:55.300
to sit alongside SAS.

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:59.580
But certainly once we've
proved the case for the R,

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:02.160
and if you go back to
that validation point,

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:04.460
you can prove that case
for R. Why not Python?

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:05.480
Why not Julia?

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:06.830
Why not other languages?

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:08.510
[INAUDIBLE] open
source space, you

00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:11.210
can start taking advantage of
some of the interoperability

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:12.080
between these tools.

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:18.100
So the two GSK catalysts
I wanted to talk about--

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:21.960
the first one is
the Shiny User Case.

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:27.150
And this is something we call
quantitative decision making

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:28.500
at GSK.

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:31.620
In a nutshell, it
is the probability

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:33.960
of being able to make a
decision at the end of the trial

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:38.470
given the operating
characteristics of the study--

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:42.510
so the study design and various
other pieces of information

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:44.180
that we know up front.

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:46.180
I don't want to talk to
this in a lot of detail.

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:48.660
Again, this is something I
have given a whole presentation

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:51.480
on in the past.

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:53.590
But essentially,
what we try and do

00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:55.740
with quantitative
decision making at GSK

00:10:55.740 --> 00:10:57.282
is we take known values.

00:10:57.282 --> 00:10:58.740
So up in the top
right-hand corner,

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:00.943
there's a diagram here
where it talks about--

00:11:00.943 --> 00:11:02.610
this is looking at
treatment difference.

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:04.560
So on the x-axis,
you've got a difference.

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:07.200
For example, the difference
between our drug and placebo,

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:09.900
our drug and some
competitor on the market.

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:14.160
And in a standard
clinical trial,

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:18.060
you might look at the difference
between those two compounds.

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:21.582
And you're going to generate
some kind of p-value.

00:11:21.582 --> 00:11:23.040
And if it's a late
phase study, you

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:25.747
might be looking for
something like p less 0.05

00:11:25.747 --> 00:11:28.080
to show that there is some
kind of treatment difference.

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:29.670
I'm not as great
at the study level,

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:31.770
but what [INAUDIBLE]
is looking at really

00:11:31.770 --> 00:11:34.050
is the lifecycle
of the compound.

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:39.300
So in the early phases of
development, so in phase 2

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:46.980
where [INAUDIBLE] is
particularly used GSK,

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:52.950
you can't [INAUDIBLE] to be able
to make those [INAUDIBLE] kind

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:53.780
of statements.

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:56.610
What you want to be able
to do is given everything

00:11:56.610 --> 00:11:58.710
you know about
the drug, you want

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:00.840
to be able to estimate
probabilities.

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:04.260
What's the probability that
the difference between the two

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:09.150
compounds is greater than
some clinically meaningful,

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:12.610
commercially viable difference?

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:15.670
Now, all of this is
quite complicated--

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:16.840
uses a bayesian framework.

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:20.537
We put priors on all
the information we know.

00:12:20.537 --> 00:12:22.870
There's a lot of study design
elements that we consider.

00:12:22.870 --> 00:12:24.495
And what we're trying
to get to the end

00:12:24.495 --> 00:12:26.502
is the probability of
success of the studies.

00:12:26.502 --> 00:12:27.960
So the probability
that we're going

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:29.740
to be able to make
a clear decision as

00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:32.860
to whether to continue into
the next phase of development

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:36.310
or stop development
of our compound.

00:12:39.348 --> 00:12:40.640
Because it's quite complicated.

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:45.740
When we train a lot of our
clinicians in the methodology,

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:47.900
they weren't immediately
receptive to everything

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:48.760
that we were saying.

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:51.190
It's a lot of
complex mathematics.

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:56.420
When mathematics is generally
not their first job.

00:12:56.420 --> 00:13:00.430
So we developed an app.

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:05.410
And in doing so,
[INAUDIBLE] all the factors.

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:08.585
Things like the sample size,
the randomization ratios,

00:13:08.585 --> 00:13:10.960
standard deviation, things
you can see on the screen here

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:14.800
hopefully, and you combine
that with information

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:17.160
about the minimum value
and the target value,

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:20.050
and potential loss of other
operating characteristics

00:13:20.050 --> 00:13:20.980
as well.

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:25.180
And you allow the
clinician teams

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:27.900
to play around with the
sliders in different options.

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:29.920
And then it starts to
become real for them.

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:32.170
They can start to see what
the impacts of study design

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:34.880
is on the ultimate
probability of success.

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:36.430
And that's really led--

00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:38.740
because this is such an
important initiative of GSK,

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:41.540
it's rolled out across
all of our studies at GSK.

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:43.450
They're in that
phase of development.

00:13:43.450 --> 00:13:50.650
And because of that, we're
seeing widespread adoption

00:13:50.650 --> 00:13:53.350
of [INAUDIBLE] methodology.

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:54.923
Everyone is seeing
the Shiny apps,

00:13:54.923 --> 00:13:57.340
and there's a huge number of
requests for additional Shiny

00:13:57.340 --> 00:13:57.840
apps.

00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:04.880
Second example of brief
[INAUDIBLE] a little bit more

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:05.380
briefly.

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:08.320
The-- is dynamic reporting.

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:11.620
Essentially a submission process
at the moment or a development

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:15.760
process is such that we
generate summary data sets

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:23.860
in a very old-fashioned,
boring text tables format

00:14:23.860 --> 00:14:24.700
at the moment.

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:26.500
And we generate
these in the same way

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:28.340
we've done for 20 or 30 years.

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:31.300
And that's fine
for R submissions.

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:35.050
But then when we want to
present those in a PowerPoint

00:14:35.050 --> 00:14:37.532
presentation, and we want
to publish those results

00:14:37.532 --> 00:14:39.490
in some other way, what
we find ourselves doing

00:14:39.490 --> 00:14:41.660
is reformatting all
that information.

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:43.870
Even in fact, in the
clinical study report,

00:14:43.870 --> 00:14:46.317
we hand over the data to
our medical writing teams.

00:14:46.317 --> 00:14:48.400
And they end up doing
things like manually merging

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:52.750
columns and reformatting some of
the data that we've given them.

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:55.210
Before it goes to the
clinical study report.

00:14:55.210 --> 00:14:58.738
So you could use-- if you had--

00:14:58.738 --> 00:15:00.280
you weren't using
Microsoft products,

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:02.822
something like R Markdown would
be equally suitable for this.

00:15:02.822 --> 00:15:06.070
We actually use the Office
package to prove this concept

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:10.690
last year that we could
just take the data sets,

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:13.870
the summary data sets that
we generated by statistics

00:15:13.870 --> 00:15:16.930
and automatically place
all of the outputs

00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:20.050
into each of these
different formats

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:22.680
without the need
for huge amounts

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:25.090
to transcription QC, which
is what takes place today.

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:30.490
So I'm just going to give
you a bit of an introduction

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:32.698
and a couple of [INAUDIBLE]----
couple of things that

00:15:32.698 --> 00:15:36.130
we're looking at that are really
accelerating the adoption of R

00:15:36.130 --> 00:15:36.685
within GSK.

00:15:39.220 --> 00:15:41.320
What I want to move
on to now is generally

00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:45.220
how we're trying to
build this capability.

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:48.640
So we've [INAUDIBLE] so I guess
some of the marketing company

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:51.480
advertising that we've done
internally to promote R

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:54.130
and in building capability,
I put out something here

00:15:54.130 --> 00:15:56.920
which will probably horrify
anyone working in change

00:15:56.920 --> 00:15:59.590
management but what I'm calling
a simple recipe for building

00:15:59.590 --> 00:16:00.250
capability.

00:16:00.250 --> 00:16:05.000
Because the components here--
the ingredients, if you like--

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:07.210
and [INAUDIBLE]
not rocket science.

00:16:07.210 --> 00:16:08.930
It's more about how you do it.

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:11.800
And that's what we'll talk about
in the rest of this webinar.

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:14.080
So Tito is going to talk
about the environment

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:16.190
that we've built about
warped environment.

00:16:16.190 --> 00:16:19.193
And Michael will
talk about the way

00:16:19.193 --> 00:16:20.860
that we're promoting
on the job learning

00:16:20.860 --> 00:16:23.140
through initiative [INAUDIBLE].

00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:24.640
Before I move on
to training I just

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:26.140
want to highlight
as well that given

00:16:26.140 --> 00:16:28.093
this is a heavily
regulated industry,

00:16:28.093 --> 00:16:30.010
processes and standards
also plays a big part.

00:16:30.010 --> 00:16:32.410
We actually have standard
operating procedures

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:36.490
that effectively
reference SAS directly--

00:16:36.490 --> 00:16:38.220
not intentionally,
but our workflows

00:16:38.220 --> 00:16:40.840
is so built around
the SAS workflow

00:16:40.840 --> 00:16:46.090
that certain elements that
don't even make sense for R.

00:16:46.090 --> 00:16:48.910
And of course, marketing,
I've mentioned.

00:16:48.910 --> 00:16:50.650
So let's move on,
look at training

00:16:50.650 --> 00:16:53.020
before I hand over
to Tito to talk you

00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:55.870
through the work environment.

00:16:55.870 --> 00:17:00.320
What you can see on the slide
now, which is a build slides,

00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:03.340
are three prongs of how
we talk about data science

00:17:03.340 --> 00:17:04.569
within biostatistics.

00:17:04.569 --> 00:17:06.819
And this is largely base
around our traditional roles.

00:17:06.819 --> 00:17:08.410
So the top, we talk
about modeling.

00:17:08.410 --> 00:17:10.480
And modeling is the
role that traditionally

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:15.250
performed by a statistician
in GSK biostatistics.

00:17:15.250 --> 00:17:19.839
And a programmer would
fulfill that sort of wrangling

00:17:19.839 --> 00:17:21.700
what was in biostatistics.

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:26.200
The role that my team
fulfills in promoting R

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:27.650
is more of an engineering role.

00:17:27.650 --> 00:17:28.930
And engineering can mean
lots of different things

00:17:28.930 --> 00:17:30.138
for lots of different people.

00:17:30.138 --> 00:17:31.990
What we mean in
this space is things

00:17:31.990 --> 00:17:38.350
like building the environments,
building applications, building

00:17:38.350 --> 00:17:41.980
all packages so if you like,
the tooling that goes to help

00:17:41.980 --> 00:17:46.922
support data science analysis.

00:17:46.922 --> 00:17:48.880
And the way we've gone
about training everybody

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:52.903
is to focus on the Tidyverse
and focus on ggplot2.

00:17:52.903 --> 00:17:54.320
So, I've written
these two things.

00:17:54.320 --> 00:17:55.990
ggplot2 is clearly
part of Tidyverse.

00:17:55.990 --> 00:17:58.150
The reason I separated
them out in this diagram

00:17:58.150 --> 00:18:00.790
is because they are two
separate face-to-face courses

00:18:00.790 --> 00:18:01.980
that we created.

00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:05.626
We should now
[INAUDIBLE] courses

00:18:05.626 --> 00:18:07.060
for everyone in biostatistics.

00:18:07.060 --> 00:18:09.310
[INAUDIBLE] everyone's got
a base level [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:09.310 --> 00:18:10.870
before we move on.

00:18:10.870 --> 00:18:14.140
We chose Tidyverse, because
of all of the reasons

00:18:14.140 --> 00:18:16.750
you'd normally choose
Tidyverse, so things

00:18:16.750 --> 00:18:19.120
like the simplicity of the
functions, the consistency,

00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:19.720
and so on.

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:21.280
But also, because
if you're trying

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:25.480
to take people who know
SAS and train them in R,

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:28.540
R is a lot more
expansive than SAS.

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:29.320
It's a language.

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:32.530
You have things like
matrices and list and so on

00:18:32.530 --> 00:18:35.290
and can get quite
complicated when you get--

00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:36.580
when you get into it.

00:18:36.580 --> 00:18:39.730
SAS on the other hand is
built around very simple flows

00:18:39.730 --> 00:18:41.480
built around data steps--

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:43.000
simple data steps in SAS.

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:45.550
And the way that that works is
very, very similar to the way

00:18:45.550 --> 00:18:50.680
that the Tidyverse is built
around data frames or tables

00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:53.472
in the Tidyverse vernacular.

00:18:53.472 --> 00:18:55.180
So that makes it a
lot easier for someone

00:18:55.180 --> 00:18:58.090
coming across from SAS to R
to pick up tables and pick up

00:18:58.090 --> 00:18:59.740
that way of working
than it does.

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:02.680
And in particular, the
[INAUDIBLE] and everything

00:19:02.680 --> 00:19:05.830
like that is very
familiar to people

00:19:05.830 --> 00:19:07.780
coming from a SAS background.

00:19:07.780 --> 00:19:09.892
So building up in
the foundations,

00:19:09.892 --> 00:19:11.350
and then in each
of the core areas,

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:14.570
we then look to strengthen
core competency.

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:19.120
So we have bespoke training
in various different forms.

00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:20.500
I put in the
right-hand side some

00:19:20.500 --> 00:19:21.625
of the methods of delivery.

00:19:21.625 --> 00:19:24.310
So this will not all be
face-to-face training.

00:19:24.310 --> 00:19:26.200
Lots of this is
guidebooks, webinars,

00:19:26.200 --> 00:19:28.120
all sorts of different
things that we

00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:29.980
do to promote the learning.

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:34.090
And now, we call stats
training using real GSK data.

00:19:34.090 --> 00:19:36.432
And programming PROC
plus here represents

00:19:36.432 --> 00:19:38.140
sort of more targeted
training and things

00:19:38.140 --> 00:19:40.810
like stringr, lubricate-- those
kind of packages, the more

00:19:40.810 --> 00:19:42.060
advanced data manipulation.

00:19:42.060 --> 00:19:43.150
And then we look at
things like function

00:19:43.150 --> 00:19:45.130
writing from an
engineering perspective.

00:19:45.130 --> 00:19:47.453
And then when the
value comes in,

00:19:47.453 --> 00:19:48.870
it's around these
kinds of things.

00:19:48.870 --> 00:19:53.960
So Shiny clearly plays a
big part of our future.

00:19:53.960 --> 00:19:56.410
So we've got some
expansive targets

00:19:56.410 --> 00:19:57.910
around building
Shiny capability.

00:19:57.910 --> 00:20:00.700
Publishing here, I'm using to
refer to things like Officer

00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:02.800
as well as our markdown,
bookdown, blockdown--

00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:05.180
those kinds of tools as well.

00:20:05.180 --> 00:20:08.050
You can see that
through this, we're

00:20:08.050 --> 00:20:13.310
building capabilities in each
direction across the group.

00:20:13.310 --> 00:20:16.100
In this particular section,
what I'm going to focus on

00:20:16.100 --> 00:20:17.930
is the environment
that we've built

00:20:17.930 --> 00:20:21.440
to support our
statisticians, programmers,

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:24.410
and data scientists within
biostatistics and increasingly

00:20:24.410 --> 00:20:27.050
now, beyond
biostatistics as well.

00:20:27.050 --> 00:20:29.600
We call this environment
warp, because it's

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:32.600
part of a wider
initiative called space.

00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:38.000
And if you want to make
that into an acronym,

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:41.670
we sometimes talk about the
Working Area for R Programming

00:20:41.670 --> 00:20:42.170
.

00:20:50.420 --> 00:20:54.410
As has been mentioned
in the introduction,

00:20:54.410 --> 00:21:00.770
the majority of our users are
clinical users, statisticians,

00:21:00.770 --> 00:21:01.970
and programmers.

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:06.380
And the kind of data they work
with-- clinical trial data--

00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:09.710
is relatively small in
the data science space.

00:21:09.710 --> 00:21:14.270
So a few hundred
subjects visiting

00:21:14.270 --> 00:21:17.570
a clinic a handful of times,
maybe less than 10 times,

00:21:17.570 --> 00:21:20.060
generating at most, in
some of our laboratory data

00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:23.060
sets, a few megabytes of data.

00:21:23.060 --> 00:21:26.660
Certainly, not generally
in the gigabyte terms.

00:21:26.660 --> 00:21:28.850
And the kind of analysis
that we're running

00:21:28.850 --> 00:21:33.110
are standard
statistical models--

00:21:33.110 --> 00:21:35.780
linear models, generalizing
models, mixed models,

00:21:35.780 --> 00:21:38.280
[INAUDIBLE],, these
kinds of things.

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:42.560
And when we simulate
that data, what we

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:45.050
have is computationally
demanding simulations

00:21:45.050 --> 00:21:47.180
where we fit that model
over and over again.

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:50.960
As opposed to needing to work
with very, very large data

00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:54.578
sources, and trying to solve
problems of memory, and so on.

00:21:54.578 --> 00:21:56.120
The only exception
to that is that we

00:21:56.120 --> 00:22:02.020
are seeing increasing use of
molecular and genomics data

00:22:02.020 --> 00:22:02.710
as well.

00:22:02.710 --> 00:22:04.120
And that's something that
we'll have to adopt soon,

00:22:04.120 --> 00:22:05.162
respond to in the future.

00:22:07.452 --> 00:22:09.910
So in terms of what that means,
the environment is a cover.

00:22:09.910 --> 00:22:11.860
Well, we need an interactive
environment at its core.

00:22:11.860 --> 00:22:13.777
And I'll talk through
each of these components

00:22:13.777 --> 00:22:15.830
in the next few
slides separately.

00:22:15.830 --> 00:22:19.250
We need a high
performance capability

00:22:19.250 --> 00:22:22.050
to support those simulations
that I mentioned.

00:22:22.050 --> 00:22:26.450
And with respect to our new
capabilities, our Shiny apps

00:22:26.450 --> 00:22:31.100
and our Markdown-based
workflows,

00:22:31.100 --> 00:22:35.020
we need an ability to
share that kind of content.

00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:41.090
And of course, to
do anything, we

00:22:41.090 --> 00:22:43.280
need access to all
of our data sources.

00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:46.250
We've provided this through
a centralized environment.

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:47.750
Probably is not a
surprise to anyone

00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:49.040
who works in this industry.

00:22:49.040 --> 00:22:51.770
But our aim is to get people
off desktops where we can

00:22:51.770 --> 00:22:53.870
and provide a level of
consistency, particularly

00:22:53.870 --> 00:22:55.470
in the clinical setting.

00:22:55.470 --> 00:22:58.100
We need consistency,
traceability,

00:22:58.100 --> 00:23:00.122
transparency in what we do.

00:23:00.122 --> 00:23:01.580
And we have a large
number of users

00:23:01.580 --> 00:23:04.880
that we need to support
when doing this.

00:23:04.880 --> 00:23:07.580
We have capacity at the moment
for around about 1,000 data

00:23:07.580 --> 00:23:12.801
scientists with 300
concurrent users we estimate.

00:23:15.875 --> 00:23:17.250
This diagram is
just to highlight

00:23:17.250 --> 00:23:18.625
some of the
technologies that are

00:23:18.625 --> 00:23:21.210
included in this environment.

00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:22.695
You'll see, as
well as all R, you

00:23:22.695 --> 00:23:25.620
see technologies like
Python and Stan mentioned.

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:28.860
Generally, we access
those through R as opposed

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:34.860
to direct access
through their own ID.

00:23:34.860 --> 00:23:38.190
So everything is built
around RStudio Server Pro.

00:23:38.190 --> 00:23:42.405
And it's very much designed
as an R-based environment.

00:23:45.620 --> 00:23:48.050
All of those other
components there are really

00:23:48.050 --> 00:23:53.390
just supporting the
use of R. So even

00:23:53.390 --> 00:23:55.940
take something like
Git and GitHub,

00:23:55.940 --> 00:23:58.340
that's actually maintained
completely separately

00:23:58.340 --> 00:24:00.260
but will provide
a connection in R

00:24:00.260 --> 00:24:05.690
to allow the code to be stored
or managed through GitHub.

00:24:08.860 --> 00:24:12.550
So we've chosen RStudio So
maybe this is an obvious choice

00:24:12.550 --> 00:24:13.450
for many people.

00:24:13.450 --> 00:24:21.510
But clearly, the RStudio ID
is the standard ID far anyway.

00:24:21.510 --> 00:24:24.100
But increasingly, in
the data science space,

00:24:24.100 --> 00:24:29.835
I do see a trend towards more
notebook-like environments.

00:24:29.835 --> 00:24:31.960
But really, in a clinical
space, that doesn't work.

00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:34.860
We work with scripts primarily.

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:38.020
And for those users
coming across from SAS,

00:24:38.020 --> 00:24:39.910
all they've ever known
really is scripts.

00:24:39.910 --> 00:24:42.340
So first and foremost,
we need an environment

00:24:42.340 --> 00:24:49.030
to be able to develop and
execute scripts and RStudio.

00:24:49.030 --> 00:24:53.530
Server Pro is really nice
in that it provides-- well,

00:24:53.530 --> 00:24:54.350
RStudio--

00:24:54.350 --> 00:24:57.190
the RStudio ID generally
provides notebook access

00:24:57.190 --> 00:25:02.860
and obviously use of our
mark down if you want it,

00:25:02.860 --> 00:25:04.780
and therefore it's
the best of both.

00:25:04.780 --> 00:25:08.560
But it also provides the
ability to load balance.

00:25:08.560 --> 00:25:10.690
So for us with our
high number of users,

00:25:10.690 --> 00:25:12.970
we actually have two
servers with RStudio Server

00:25:12.970 --> 00:25:16.990
Pro running, and we--

00:25:16.990 --> 00:25:19.770
users will log onto
a single address,

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:21.310
and they'll be
put onto whichever

00:25:21.310 --> 00:25:23.507
server has the most capacity.

00:25:23.507 --> 00:25:25.090
And this is also
really, really useful

00:25:25.090 --> 00:25:27.610
for things like maintenance
to minimize the downtime

00:25:27.610 --> 00:25:30.462
that users experience, as well.

00:25:30.462 --> 00:25:32.920
There are some challenges with
the interactive environment,

00:25:32.920 --> 00:25:35.337
the load balancing with respect
to high performance usage,

00:25:35.337 --> 00:25:38.848
but I'll come onto that
after the next slide.

00:25:38.848 --> 00:25:40.640
So in this slide, what
I want to talk about

00:25:40.640 --> 00:25:45.050
is our means to manage
such an environment.

00:25:45.050 --> 00:25:48.410
So for a regulatory
environment, I

00:25:48.410 --> 00:25:51.860
suppose at the moment
we are quite open.

00:25:51.860 --> 00:25:55.820
So we're not locking
much down for our users.

00:25:55.820 --> 00:25:59.360
First and foremost, we need to
get people off laptops with R.

00:25:59.360 --> 00:26:03.260
And on a laptop, they're used
to high levels of flexibility.

00:26:03.260 --> 00:26:06.920
So we provide a central
installation of R,

00:26:06.920 --> 00:26:12.180
and that installation is
updated every six months.

00:26:12.180 --> 00:26:13.880
So we have many,
many versions of R,

00:26:13.880 --> 00:26:17.980
and all of the recent
versions are exposed to users.

00:26:17.980 --> 00:26:22.280
So for every six months over
a period of three years,

00:26:22.280 --> 00:26:24.830
you can go back and
select a version of R

00:26:24.830 --> 00:26:26.337
that you want to use.

00:26:26.337 --> 00:26:27.920
And when we talk
about a version of R,

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:30.370
we don't just mean the core
and recommended packages.

00:26:30.370 --> 00:26:35.120
To date, we currently have
around 450 packages centrally

00:26:35.120 --> 00:26:37.070
installed in a system
library, as well.

00:26:37.070 --> 00:26:40.220
So when a new user
logs onto the system,

00:26:40.220 --> 00:26:46.700
they get access to tidyverse
and other popular packages

00:26:46.700 --> 00:26:49.828
for our kind of
work immediately.

00:26:49.828 --> 00:26:51.870
They don't have to go and
install those packages.

00:26:51.870 --> 00:26:53.782
And moreover, because
these are essentially

00:26:53.782 --> 00:26:55.490
installed in the system
library, everyone

00:26:55.490 --> 00:26:58.070
has the same version of
those packages, which ensures

00:26:58.070 --> 00:27:01.460
a degree of stability in usage.

00:27:01.460 --> 00:27:03.050
We do allow
flexibility, so users

00:27:03.050 --> 00:27:04.730
can install their own
packages, as well,

00:27:04.730 --> 00:27:07.108
in their own personal
user library.

00:27:07.108 --> 00:27:08.900
We're not restricted
in that at the moment,

00:27:08.900 --> 00:27:12.200
but it's very likely that we'll
supplement this environment

00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:18.860
in the future with more static,
stable versions of R that

00:27:18.860 --> 00:27:20.500
are a bit more locked down.

00:27:20.500 --> 00:27:22.250
The only element of
control that we really

00:27:22.250 --> 00:27:25.070
have on the environment is
provided through RStudio

00:27:25.070 --> 00:27:26.420
Package Manager.

00:27:26.420 --> 00:27:33.860
So RStudio Package Manager
provides access to packages

00:27:33.860 --> 00:27:35.810
that users would
typically go and install

00:27:35.810 --> 00:27:39.020
from CRAN, Bioconductor,
GitHub and so on.

00:27:39.020 --> 00:27:41.733
And with respect to
CRAN and Bioconductor,

00:27:41.733 --> 00:27:43.400
we pretty much provide
the whole of CRAN

00:27:43.400 --> 00:27:46.370
and-- well, we do provide the
whole of CRAN and Bioconductor.

00:27:46.370 --> 00:27:49.130
For GitHub, users
would have to request

00:27:49.130 --> 00:27:53.232
that particular repositories
are added to our environment

00:27:53.232 --> 00:27:54.440
before they can install that.

00:27:54.440 --> 00:27:57.680
So they can't directly access
CRAN, Bioconductor, or GitHub,

00:27:57.680 --> 00:27:59.370
or anywhere else.

00:27:59.370 --> 00:28:02.240
And we also have our
own GitHub enterprise

00:28:02.240 --> 00:28:05.600
at GSK, as well, which we
release packages on to,

00:28:05.600 --> 00:28:07.580
and users can use
RStudio Package

00:28:07.580 --> 00:28:10.180
Manager to access those.

00:28:10.180 --> 00:28:12.680
What's actually really, really
nice about our studio package

00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:15.590
manager is that it allows
us to monitor the downloads.

00:28:15.590 --> 00:28:19.070
And so when we see users
installing-- many users

00:28:19.070 --> 00:28:22.970
installing the same
package, what we can then do

00:28:22.970 --> 00:28:25.070
is to install that
in the central system

00:28:25.070 --> 00:28:27.560
library in a future release.

00:28:27.560 --> 00:28:30.110
Again, just to limit the
amount of personalization

00:28:30.110 --> 00:28:33.890
and customization that might
affect the transferability

00:28:33.890 --> 00:28:37.048
of our code.

00:28:37.048 --> 00:28:39.340
And speaking of central
monitoring, another really nice

00:28:39.340 --> 00:28:41.865
feature that is the--

00:28:41.865 --> 00:28:46.330
that admin support team
use quite extensively

00:28:46.330 --> 00:28:53.300
is the admin screens that are
available through R Studio--

00:28:53.300 --> 00:28:55.580
through the RStudio Server Pro.

00:28:55.580 --> 00:28:59.560
So here you can see
a view of our system.

00:28:59.560 --> 00:29:02.680
What I haven't mentioned
thus far is that the system--

00:29:02.680 --> 00:29:05.410
these two servers that we
use are very, very powerful

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:05.910
servers.

00:29:05.910 --> 00:29:08.368
They're originally intended
for high performance computing,

00:29:08.368 --> 00:29:10.570
so they each have 192
cores and 3 terabytes

00:29:10.570 --> 00:29:13.330
of RAM, which is why
you can see sort of 1%

00:29:13.330 --> 00:29:16.700
CPU memory usage on here.

00:29:16.700 --> 00:29:20.200
So generally, we are not pushing
these servers to their limits.

00:29:20.200 --> 00:29:22.450
What you can see, though,
are these little spikes that

00:29:22.450 --> 00:29:24.220
occur here, and these are--

00:29:24.220 --> 00:29:28.300
these spikes occur when a
user tries to paralyze--

00:29:28.300 --> 00:29:30.490
or does successfully
paralyze code

00:29:30.490 --> 00:29:34.990
and executes that
in the background.

00:29:34.990 --> 00:29:38.170
So we can monitor the
system, see these spikes,

00:29:38.170 --> 00:29:40.090
and then talk to those users.

00:29:40.090 --> 00:29:44.043
Users are permitted
to generate--

00:29:44.043 --> 00:29:45.460
they are allowed
to paralyze code,

00:29:45.460 --> 00:29:47.380
and they are allowed
to practice HPC runs,

00:29:47.380 --> 00:29:49.088
but our expectation
is that they run them

00:29:49.088 --> 00:29:53.250
on a separate HPC system,
which I'll talk through next.

00:29:56.090 --> 00:30:00.020
So that system is fully
accessible from our R Studio

00:30:00.020 --> 00:30:01.970
Server Pro environment.

00:30:01.970 --> 00:30:07.910
So we built an add-in
to access Slurm,

00:30:07.910 --> 00:30:09.680
which is the tool
that we use the--

00:30:09.680 --> 00:30:12.530
for accessing our HPC.

00:30:12.530 --> 00:30:15.780
So you can see on here each one
of these is a different server.

00:30:15.780 --> 00:30:20.660
So when the users in R Studio
Server Pro access that add in,

00:30:20.660 --> 00:30:21.230
that--

00:30:21.230 --> 00:30:23.063
the information they
enter there essentially

00:30:23.063 --> 00:30:27.500
writes the necessary
script to launch that job

00:30:27.500 --> 00:30:30.950
on to two separate HPC nodes.

00:30:30.950 --> 00:30:32.600
Again, all of these nodes--

00:30:32.600 --> 00:30:34.642
every single one of these
servers in this diagram

00:30:34.642 --> 00:30:38.660
has 190 cores and 3 terabytes
of memory, which is far too

00:30:38.660 --> 00:30:40.400
much for our management node.

00:30:40.400 --> 00:30:43.055
It doesn't need
anything like that.

00:30:43.055 --> 00:30:44.930
And no doubt we'll change
that in the future,

00:30:44.930 --> 00:30:47.420
but certainly for
the HPC that means

00:30:47.420 --> 00:30:50.272
we've got some very
powerful servers available.

00:30:50.272 --> 00:30:51.980
One point, though, I
mentioned that we've

00:30:51.980 --> 00:30:53.750
built an add-in for this.

00:30:53.750 --> 00:30:58.820
We were sort of bleeding
edge when we were doing this,

00:30:58.820 --> 00:31:03.770
and the functionality wasn't
readily available in R Studio.

00:31:03.770 --> 00:31:06.380
I understand that
it is available now.

00:31:06.380 --> 00:31:09.750
You can use launcher
within R Studio

00:31:09.750 --> 00:31:12.980
to work with Slurm,
as well as Kubernetes,

00:31:12.980 --> 00:31:19.160
but until GSK migrates to
start using Kubernetes,

00:31:19.160 --> 00:31:23.390
we are still using own custom
add-in at this moment in time.

00:31:29.113 --> 00:31:30.530
So beyond the
obvious, I just want

00:31:30.530 --> 00:31:35.030
to now highlight the value add,
I guess, of this environment--

00:31:35.030 --> 00:31:37.880
so the new things that we can do
now that we couldn't already do

00:31:37.880 --> 00:31:40.430
in our SAS based environments.

00:31:40.430 --> 00:31:46.940
And for content sharing--
so for sharing Shiny apps,

00:31:46.940 --> 00:31:51.530
or Markdown documents,
Bookdown, Blogdown,

00:31:51.530 --> 00:31:55.770
we use RStudio Connect.

00:31:55.770 --> 00:31:59.290
We have one server at the
moment running RStudio Connect.

00:31:59.290 --> 00:32:00.800
In future, again,
that might be two,

00:32:00.800 --> 00:32:04.520
so we might evolve to have
a development and production

00:32:04.520 --> 00:32:05.180
server.

00:32:05.180 --> 00:32:06.650
But at the moment,
it's all in one,

00:32:06.650 --> 00:32:08.060
and it's a pretty liberal model.

00:32:08.060 --> 00:32:11.110
So users can publish
anything they like.

00:32:11.110 --> 00:32:13.190
So you see a lot of apps--

00:32:13.190 --> 00:32:16.790
development apps with the
word test next to them.

00:32:16.790 --> 00:32:19.820
Primarily, I'd say
the Shiny apps are--

00:32:19.820 --> 00:32:21.630
I mean, the Shiny
apps are a mixed bag.

00:32:21.630 --> 00:32:24.850
We get all sorts-- the QDM,
quantity decision making app

00:32:24.850 --> 00:32:26.660
that I mentioned earlier.

00:32:26.660 --> 00:32:31.130
We get various dashboards
that people have created,

00:32:31.130 --> 00:32:33.130
training apps, and so on.

00:32:33.130 --> 00:32:34.580
The image in the
left hand side is

00:32:34.580 --> 00:32:37.190
taken from a proof
of concept we did

00:32:37.190 --> 00:32:44.760
to use Shiny to report
clinical trial data.

00:32:44.760 --> 00:32:47.690
So we've got an ongoing
initiative at the moment

00:32:47.690 --> 00:32:51.410
where we're looking at replacing
a lot of our standard outputs

00:32:51.410 --> 00:32:53.960
with a Shiny app, and there's
a lot of questions that

00:32:53.960 --> 00:32:57.470
come with that around
things like data access

00:32:57.470 --> 00:33:03.290
and what to do if we find
anything in there when

00:33:03.290 --> 00:33:04.400
we're exploring the data.

00:33:04.400 --> 00:33:05.317
How do we report that?

00:33:05.317 --> 00:33:06.280
And so on.

00:33:06.280 --> 00:33:07.947
So there are a lot
of questions in that,

00:33:07.947 --> 00:33:11.140
but we're starting to explore
Shiny for that, as well.

00:33:11.140 --> 00:33:13.890
Otherwise, we also
use RStudio Connect

00:33:13.890 --> 00:33:16.890
for all of our
training material.

00:33:16.890 --> 00:33:19.320
So most of those are
produced using Bookdown,

00:33:19.320 --> 00:33:20.745
and we host those on Connect.

00:33:20.745 --> 00:33:23.370
One particular feature I want to
highlight that isn't mentioned

00:33:23.370 --> 00:33:24.787
on this slide, but
it is something

00:33:24.787 --> 00:33:28.080
that I'm really,
really thrilled about

00:33:28.080 --> 00:33:32.280
is there's a feature in
RStudio Connect that allows you

00:33:32.280 --> 00:33:34.590
to publish directly from Git.

00:33:34.590 --> 00:33:37.170
So we use Git and
GitHub extensively,

00:33:37.170 --> 00:33:39.030
particularly within my team.

00:33:39.030 --> 00:33:44.940
And you can point
RStudio Connect directly

00:33:44.940 --> 00:33:47.730
at a particular
branch on GitHub.

00:33:47.730 --> 00:33:51.180
So for example, we'd
have our production apps

00:33:51.180 --> 00:33:54.092
pointing at the
master branch, and we

00:33:54.092 --> 00:33:57.510
would have a test
branch where we

00:33:57.510 --> 00:34:00.630
would point RStudio
Connect at for more

00:34:00.630 --> 00:34:05.263
regular updates and testing.

00:34:05.263 --> 00:34:06.930
And that's a really,
really nice feature

00:34:06.930 --> 00:34:08.888
that often doesn't get
talked about that I just

00:34:08.888 --> 00:34:11.280
wanted to highlight.

00:34:11.280 --> 00:34:15.850
Otherwise, I think RStudio
Connect generally works really,

00:34:15.850 --> 00:34:17.040
really well for us.

00:34:17.040 --> 00:34:19.139
The only bit of
guidance that we often

00:34:19.139 --> 00:34:23.370
need to work with our users
on is around data access.

00:34:23.370 --> 00:34:26.670
So sometimes people
don't understand

00:34:26.670 --> 00:34:28.020
how RStudio Connect runs.

00:34:28.020 --> 00:34:31.080
You can basically
configure it to run as you,

00:34:31.080 --> 00:34:33.300
as the user that's trying
to access that content,

00:34:33.300 --> 00:34:36.639
or you can configure it
to run as a generic user.

00:34:36.639 --> 00:34:39.116
And that affects things
like data access.

00:34:39.116 --> 00:34:40.949
And so we're often
having to work with users

00:34:40.949 --> 00:34:43.560
to explain that.

00:34:43.560 --> 00:34:46.550
Equally, you can configure--
you can design an app such

00:34:46.550 --> 00:34:49.000
that the data is deployed
as part of the app,

00:34:49.000 --> 00:34:52.260
or you can write apps to access
external data sources where

00:34:52.260 --> 00:34:56.429
some of what I just mentioned
becomes more of an issue.

00:34:56.429 --> 00:34:58.160
But there's lots of
different mechanisms

00:34:58.160 --> 00:35:01.530
for controlling
access within Connect,

00:35:01.530 --> 00:35:03.750
and that's one of the
key advantages, I think,

00:35:03.750 --> 00:35:05.955
that we see over it for Shiny--

00:35:05.955 --> 00:35:07.830
we see for it for Shiny
apps-- is making sure

00:35:07.830 --> 00:35:09.663
that the right people
see the right content.

00:35:11.585 --> 00:35:13.210
Just before we round
off, I just wanted

00:35:13.210 --> 00:35:14.620
to mention some of the back end.

00:35:14.620 --> 00:35:20.170
So I've mentioned things
like relational databases.

00:35:20.170 --> 00:35:23.435
These are ideal for
clinical trial data.

00:35:23.435 --> 00:35:25.810
Our clinical trial data is
actually stored on file shares

00:35:25.810 --> 00:35:28.420
at the moment, but
in future [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:28.420 --> 00:35:30.820
on a relational database
or a graph database.

00:35:30.820 --> 00:35:34.210
So we've provided access to a
number of internal file shares,

00:35:34.210 --> 00:35:38.290
and we're able to access
relation databases, as well.

00:35:40.800 --> 00:35:43.910
And at the bottom of the slide
is mentioned Hadoop clusters.

00:35:43.910 --> 00:35:47.820
Outside of our sort
of clinical setting,

00:35:47.820 --> 00:35:50.180
there are a number of users
who want to access things

00:35:50.180 --> 00:35:53.510
like real world data, electronic
health record databases that

00:35:53.510 --> 00:35:57.500
are stored on separate
Hadoop clusters within GSK.

00:35:57.500 --> 00:35:59.270
So we provide access to those.

00:35:59.270 --> 00:36:01.940
And then the second one
down on the slides--

00:36:01.940 --> 00:36:03.680
the objects store that we have.

00:36:03.680 --> 00:36:05.360
Again, something
that's perhaps not

00:36:05.360 --> 00:36:11.330
talked about too much
these days, but I think

00:36:11.330 --> 00:36:13.580
we will see it a little
bit more in the future

00:36:13.580 --> 00:36:16.670
is the concept of
an object store--

00:36:16.670 --> 00:36:17.660
the data.

00:36:17.660 --> 00:36:20.740
And we have some
very large structured

00:36:20.740 --> 00:36:22.700
and unstructured
data sources that we

00:36:22.700 --> 00:36:24.090
access through object store.

00:36:24.090 --> 00:36:27.290
And it's something
that's helped us develop

00:36:27.290 --> 00:36:33.230
our first clinical RNA
sequencing pipeline, which

00:36:33.230 --> 00:36:38.180
we'll be able to announce later
this year, as well as provide--

00:36:38.180 --> 00:36:43.190
as well as providing a means
to enable another pipeline that

00:36:43.190 --> 00:36:49.460
allowed us to
analyze COVID-19 data

00:36:49.460 --> 00:36:55.480
and respond to a demand from
our clinical operations group

00:36:55.480 --> 00:36:58.670
to try and understand what
the impacts of COVID-19

00:36:58.670 --> 00:37:01.190
is going to be on clinical
trial recruitment.

00:37:01.190 --> 00:37:03.680
So there's been a couple
of really nice use cases

00:37:03.680 --> 00:37:06.350
where connecting to object store
has been particularly powerful,

00:37:06.350 --> 00:37:09.590
and we're very grateful for the
work that's gone into getting

00:37:09.590 --> 00:37:13.800
AWSS3 back onto CRAN.

00:37:13.800 --> 00:37:17.113
So I'll just finish with an
overview of the whole system.

00:37:17.113 --> 00:37:18.905
I've spoken about each
of these components.

00:37:18.905 --> 00:37:22.110
The only one really to highlight
that I haven't mentioned before

00:37:22.110 --> 00:37:24.360
is this management in any--

00:37:24.360 --> 00:37:26.890
I haven't mentioned in any
detail is this management node.

00:37:26.890 --> 00:37:30.240
So this contains our
centralized R distribution.

00:37:30.240 --> 00:37:33.390
So whether it's Connect, whether
it's interact environments,

00:37:33.390 --> 00:37:35.190
or HPC nodes, all of
these are working off

00:37:35.190 --> 00:37:38.940
the same base R
installation which

00:37:38.940 --> 00:37:41.228
sits on there, as
well as things like R

00:37:41.228 --> 00:37:43.020
Studio Package Manager
then to manage that,

00:37:43.020 --> 00:37:45.570
and the Slurm workload manager.

00:37:45.570 --> 00:37:48.070
So having this extra node
independent from the rest

00:37:48.070 --> 00:37:49.210
is kind of useful.

00:37:49.210 --> 00:37:52.772
It's not necessary as such to
have it is a separate node.

00:37:52.772 --> 00:37:54.480
And as I mentioned in
the previous slide,

00:37:54.480 --> 00:37:57.510
it's a little bit overpowered at
the moment for what we need it

00:37:57.510 --> 00:37:59.723
for, but it's a really,
really useful component,

00:37:59.723 --> 00:38:01.140
as well as the
load balancing that

00:38:01.140 --> 00:38:03.480
takes place everywhere else.

00:38:03.480 --> 00:38:06.330
And as mentioned, a
future improvement

00:38:06.330 --> 00:38:11.410
will be to balance the content
across the Connect servers,

00:38:11.410 --> 00:38:11.910
as well.

00:38:14.820 --> 00:38:19.130
So with that, I will round
off and hand over to Michael

00:38:19.130 --> 00:38:22.730
to talk about our
on the job learning,

00:38:22.730 --> 00:38:27.680
so an initiative called
R4QC in particular.

00:38:27.680 --> 00:38:28.640
Thank you very much.

00:38:28.640 --> 00:38:30.598
MICHAEL RIMLER: Yeah, my
name's Michael Rimler.

00:38:30.598 --> 00:38:33.470
I am in clinical programming
within GSK biostatistics,

00:38:33.470 --> 00:38:38.990
and I am here to talk about R
adoption story, or our adoption

00:38:38.990 --> 00:38:41.330
story, within
clinical programming

00:38:41.330 --> 00:38:46.400
and really in the clinical
reporting process.

00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:50.080
The objectives of this project
really are kind of twofold.

00:38:50.080 --> 00:38:53.840
At top level, one is
driving capability.

00:38:53.840 --> 00:38:57.510
So as Andy mentioned, we have--

00:38:57.510 --> 00:39:02.967
a vast majority of our staff are
historically SAS programmers,

00:39:02.967 --> 00:39:04.550
but we're moving
into this space where

00:39:04.550 --> 00:39:07.590
we want to integrate R into
the clinical reporting process.

00:39:07.590 --> 00:39:09.290
And to do that, we
need that capability.

00:39:09.290 --> 00:39:11.570
We need people to be
able to program in R.

00:39:11.570 --> 00:39:15.440
And although people have
developed their skills over--

00:39:15.440 --> 00:39:17.030
their SAS skills over time--

00:39:17.030 --> 00:39:20.510
I've been in the industry
for 10, 11 years now,

00:39:20.510 --> 00:39:24.410
and virtually everything that
I've learned in terms of SAS

00:39:24.410 --> 00:39:28.130
has been on the job, where I
had a challenge that I needed

00:39:28.130 --> 00:39:30.260
to figure out how to resolve.

00:39:30.260 --> 00:39:31.695
And I asked questions.

00:39:31.695 --> 00:39:33.320
I looked things up,
and I figured out--

00:39:33.320 --> 00:39:35.960
it wasn't from
sitting in classes,

00:39:35.960 --> 00:39:39.560
and taking courses, and working
on problem sets, and things

00:39:39.560 --> 00:39:40.705
like that.

00:39:40.705 --> 00:39:42.080
And so we want to
be able to-- we

00:39:42.080 --> 00:39:43.897
want to be able to
build that capability.

00:39:43.897 --> 00:39:45.980
And secondly, we want to
establish an environment.

00:39:45.980 --> 00:39:47.522
And by environment,
I don't mean what

00:39:47.522 --> 00:39:49.920
Tilo was saying environment,
although that, of course,

00:39:49.920 --> 00:39:53.000
is very, very important,
but really making sure

00:39:53.000 --> 00:39:57.410
that people have the right
to, the permission to engage

00:39:57.410 --> 00:40:01.640
in certain activities in the
clinical reporting process.

00:40:01.640 --> 00:40:03.950
We know what the ways of
working are, things like that,

00:40:03.950 --> 00:40:06.383
so that someone
comes in, and then

00:40:06.383 --> 00:40:08.550
has the tools available to
them to do that, as well.

00:40:08.550 --> 00:40:12.980
So someone comes in, and we hire
them in, and apples to apples,

00:40:12.980 --> 00:40:15.290
they look the same as the
newly hired SAS programmer,

00:40:15.290 --> 00:40:18.050
but they happen to know
R, as Andy mentioned,

00:40:18.050 --> 00:40:23.060
that this is where a lot of the
folks coming out of university

00:40:23.060 --> 00:40:26.060
are coming in with that they
can be just as productive.

00:40:26.060 --> 00:40:28.710
They don't have to go through
additional steps of learning

00:40:28.710 --> 00:40:31.610
and additional training so
that they can be productive.

00:40:31.610 --> 00:40:34.070
They can be productive
right out of the gate.

00:40:34.070 --> 00:40:36.853
Along this process, we've been
asking questions like, well,

00:40:36.853 --> 00:40:37.770
why are we doing this?

00:40:37.770 --> 00:40:40.160
What's the role of R
versus SAS in our future?

00:40:40.160 --> 00:40:43.160
And to Andy's point, that
also brings into account

00:40:43.160 --> 00:40:45.620
more open source, but
here we're focusing on R.

00:40:45.620 --> 00:40:47.970
So what is the role
of R versus SAS?

00:40:47.970 --> 00:40:50.920
Now, my perspective in sort
of leading this project--

00:40:50.920 --> 00:40:52.130
and I have the--

00:40:52.130 --> 00:40:54.830
I feel like I have the support
of our senior management--

00:40:54.830 --> 00:40:57.935
is that it's
integrating R in SAS.

00:40:57.935 --> 00:40:59.060
It's not about flipping.

00:40:59.060 --> 00:41:00.770
A switch and doing R or SAS.

00:41:00.770 --> 00:41:04.563
It's about how do we integrate
this into our processes.

00:41:04.563 --> 00:41:05.480
How are we doing this?

00:41:05.480 --> 00:41:07.480
What are the elements
that are needed to declare

00:41:07.480 --> 00:41:09.060
our teams to be R ready?

00:41:09.060 --> 00:41:10.490
And what's next?

00:41:10.490 --> 00:41:13.460
So SAS has a certain
amount of capabilities.

00:41:13.460 --> 00:41:17.120
Other languages have certain
things that they excel at,

00:41:17.120 --> 00:41:19.160
but the fact that we're
trying to integrate R

00:41:19.160 --> 00:41:20.870
into our processes--
does that open up

00:41:20.870 --> 00:41:24.350
the door for our
department to be delivering

00:41:24.350 --> 00:41:27.500
other things to our,
quote unquote, customers

00:41:27.500 --> 00:41:30.860
beyond our conventional
data sets, tables, listings,

00:41:30.860 --> 00:41:32.870
and figures?

00:41:32.870 --> 00:41:35.450
This started with
a project that was

00:41:35.450 --> 00:41:38.840
called R4QC, where
we wanted to look

00:41:38.840 --> 00:41:42.960
at using R for the QC process.

00:41:42.960 --> 00:41:47.540
So for those that are unfamiliar
with a somewhat conventional QC

00:41:47.540 --> 00:41:51.248
process within
clinical reporting,

00:41:51.248 --> 00:41:52.790
you have a production
side programmer

00:41:52.790 --> 00:41:53.748
who creates the output.

00:41:53.748 --> 00:41:54.790
They create the data set.

00:41:54.790 --> 00:41:56.570
They create the table,
that sort of thing.

00:41:56.570 --> 00:41:59.060
And then someone else,
an independent programmer

00:41:59.060 --> 00:42:02.600
independently creates code
using the same inputs, but then

00:42:02.600 --> 00:42:05.010
a different process, because
independently generated.

00:42:05.010 --> 00:42:06.950
And if they get the
same outcome at the end,

00:42:06.950 --> 00:42:09.020
then that satisfies
a significant level

00:42:09.020 --> 00:42:11.850
of the requirements from
a validation perspective.

00:42:11.850 --> 00:42:13.760
And so that's what
we were looking

00:42:13.760 --> 00:42:15.360
at when the R4QC project.

00:42:15.360 --> 00:42:20.030
In parts one and two, we focused
on displays, just looking at--

00:42:20.030 --> 00:42:21.890
not looking at data sets.

00:42:21.890 --> 00:42:23.690
And it was more of a
proof of feasibility,

00:42:23.690 --> 00:42:25.370
proof of concept type model.

00:42:25.370 --> 00:42:30.170
We only did about nine
displays in part one,

00:42:30.170 --> 00:42:32.120
and then we expanded
the team, and expanded,

00:42:32.120 --> 00:42:36.620
and actually, in part two,
we were able to replicate

00:42:36.620 --> 00:42:46.810
or doubly validate and
compare to the production side

00:42:46.810 --> 00:42:51.280
SAS generated displays
using R, as opposed to SAS.

00:42:51.280 --> 00:42:53.110
It was about 150-some
displays that

00:42:53.110 --> 00:42:55.890
were in that proof of concept.

00:42:55.890 --> 00:42:58.122
That opened the door
for our part three,

00:42:58.122 --> 00:42:59.580
where we sort of
go live, which was

00:42:59.580 --> 00:43:01.080
one of our initial
objectives, which

00:43:01.080 --> 00:43:03.990
is how do we open the
door for all of our staff

00:43:03.990 --> 00:43:07.260
to be able to use R, as
opposed to SAS, for something?

00:43:07.260 --> 00:43:10.350
Because that allows them to take
any training that Andy's group

00:43:10.350 --> 00:43:13.020
is providing and
use it on the job

00:43:13.020 --> 00:43:15.840
so that they can
immediately go out and do

00:43:15.840 --> 00:43:18.240
their normal everyday
work, but start

00:43:18.240 --> 00:43:19.730
to do it in the
language of R, as

00:43:19.730 --> 00:43:21.780
opposed to the language of SAS.

00:43:21.780 --> 00:43:23.370
And so we had a
number of components,

00:43:23.370 --> 00:43:25.110
which I'll go through,
that, in order

00:43:25.110 --> 00:43:28.000
to get endorsement for
being able to do that,

00:43:28.000 --> 00:43:28.890
but we are now--

00:43:28.890 --> 00:43:32.460
our part three has now been
ongoing for about four or five

00:43:32.460 --> 00:43:36.750
months now, and we continue
to bring more and more people

00:43:36.750 --> 00:43:37.770
into the fold.

00:43:37.770 --> 00:43:39.960
At the same time, or roughly
around the same time,

00:43:39.960 --> 00:43:41.335
we launched into
part four, which

00:43:41.335 --> 00:43:43.050
is a proof of
concept that moves us

00:43:43.050 --> 00:43:47.490
into the data sets and
[INAUDIBLE] data set mapping.

00:43:47.490 --> 00:43:51.000
These two projects
now have become

00:43:51.000 --> 00:43:53.760
under the umbrella of a sort
of new and broader initiative

00:43:53.760 --> 00:43:56.400
within clinical
programming on R adoption,

00:43:56.400 --> 00:43:59.520
and there I was sort
of tasked with what's

00:43:59.520 --> 00:44:02.070
the roadmap to R then look
like for clinical programming?

00:44:02.070 --> 00:44:04.920
What is it-- what do we need
to do to get to the point

00:44:04.920 --> 00:44:07.537
where we are using R as much
as we are SAS, or at least

00:44:07.537 --> 00:44:09.120
they're sort of
interchangeable, or we

00:44:09.120 --> 00:44:10.245
have a strategic direction?

00:44:10.245 --> 00:44:11.280
That sort of thing.

00:44:11.280 --> 00:44:14.118
And before I could figure out a
roadmap, which is when are you

00:44:14.118 --> 00:44:14.910
going to do things?

00:44:14.910 --> 00:44:16.880
I needed to know what
we're going to do.

00:44:16.880 --> 00:44:19.140
What are the thing--
what are the components

00:44:19.140 --> 00:44:22.020
of broad adoption of R
within the department?

00:44:22.020 --> 00:44:25.950
And that created these
sort of six or seven work

00:44:25.950 --> 00:44:29.310
streams, which included
the R4QC remaining

00:44:29.310 --> 00:44:31.170
elements of part
three and part four,

00:44:31.170 --> 00:44:33.150
but looked at things
like central tools.

00:44:33.150 --> 00:44:38.550
So in the SAS world, in
the SAS workflow, we have--

00:44:38.550 --> 00:44:40.110
many organizations do.

00:44:40.110 --> 00:44:44.340
We have our SAS macro library
suite that people use.

00:44:44.340 --> 00:44:47.220
We don't have the same thing in
R. There's packages out there.

00:44:47.220 --> 00:44:50.490
Those packages are not written
for the clinical data pipeline,

00:44:50.490 --> 00:44:52.380
but they are-- they
do have functionality.

00:44:52.380 --> 00:44:59.850
So what can we do to make the
ability for uptake of our SAS

00:44:59.850 --> 00:45:01.500
programmers moving
into R easier?

00:45:01.500 --> 00:45:04.290
Because we're not asking them
to program for first principles.

00:45:04.290 --> 00:45:07.000
We give them a set of tools
that allows them to do that.

00:45:07.000 --> 00:45:09.180
And Andy's group on
that engineering side

00:45:09.180 --> 00:45:10.410
that he was talking about--

00:45:10.410 --> 00:45:14.460
Andy's group provided
those initial packages

00:45:14.460 --> 00:45:17.760
that we've been using
through the R4QC project.

00:45:17.760 --> 00:45:20.700
There's also a work stream on,
of course, training and support

00:45:20.700 --> 00:45:21.780
for our staff.

00:45:21.780 --> 00:45:23.370
It's a massive
organization, and this

00:45:23.370 --> 00:45:26.010
is a big change in terms
of the way we do our work.

00:45:26.010 --> 00:45:28.400
So there's a change management
and a communications piece

00:45:28.400 --> 00:45:29.300
to this.

00:45:29.300 --> 00:45:30.150
There's outreach.

00:45:30.150 --> 00:45:32.547
That's outreach--
we're really driving

00:45:32.547 --> 00:45:34.380
this within the clinical
program department,

00:45:34.380 --> 00:45:37.260
but there's outreach outside
of clinical programming still

00:45:37.260 --> 00:45:39.960
within biostats and
still within GSK,

00:45:39.960 --> 00:45:44.430
but also external outreach-- for
example, this type of platform,

00:45:44.430 --> 00:45:47.100
where we're sharing our
knowledge and our experience.

00:45:47.100 --> 00:45:49.410
We're not the first
ones to go through this,

00:45:49.410 --> 00:45:52.560
but we are carving
our own path, and we

00:45:52.560 --> 00:45:53.940
do have different experiences.

00:45:53.940 --> 00:45:55.950
And the more that we
share, the more that we

00:45:55.950 --> 00:45:58.500
can pull the rest of the
industry along with us,

00:45:58.500 --> 00:46:01.950
along with the folks
like Genentech,

00:46:01.950 --> 00:46:04.860
and Roche, and
the likes of that.

00:46:04.860 --> 00:46:07.800
And then, of course, we always
are thinking about, well,

00:46:07.800 --> 00:46:09.930
we're doing this on
the QC side of things.

00:46:09.930 --> 00:46:11.815
What does it mean to
get R onto production?

00:46:11.815 --> 00:46:13.440
Some of that falls
within Andy's group.

00:46:13.440 --> 00:46:15.857
Some of that falls within the
tech group, and some of that

00:46:15.857 --> 00:46:18.110
falls within the work
that we're doing.

00:46:18.110 --> 00:46:20.640
And there's always
the question out there

00:46:20.640 --> 00:46:27.140
about submission with R
generated analysis and data.

00:46:27.140 --> 00:46:29.310
So that R4QC project--

00:46:29.310 --> 00:46:30.990
I talked about it a
little bit already,

00:46:30.990 --> 00:46:32.990
and Andy's also
mentioned some of this

00:46:32.990 --> 00:46:39.020
also, but why do we decide
to use R in the R4QC project?

00:46:39.020 --> 00:46:43.400
We have this long term objective
within clinical programming

00:46:43.400 --> 00:46:45.710
to become programmatically
multilingual.

00:46:45.710 --> 00:46:48.492
It's not driven by
SAS license costs.

00:46:48.492 --> 00:46:50.450
Andy already mentioned
the idea of recruitment.

00:46:50.450 --> 00:46:52.220
I also think that
flips to retention

00:46:52.220 --> 00:46:55.610
for the folks that have
been within our group

00:46:55.610 --> 00:46:56.780
for a long time.

00:46:56.780 --> 00:46:58.490
This offers new
and exciting things

00:46:58.490 --> 00:47:02.090
that can do-- if you have
interest in doing that.

00:47:02.090 --> 00:47:03.980
But operationally,
it also gives us

00:47:03.980 --> 00:47:06.230
flexibility, the ability
to choose the right tool

00:47:06.230 --> 00:47:09.620
for the job at hand, and also
an expansion of the things

00:47:09.620 --> 00:47:14.610
that we might deliver to
our internal customers.

00:47:14.610 --> 00:47:16.530
Why did we look at QC?

00:47:16.530 --> 00:47:17.980
It's a lower risk
of entry point.

00:47:17.980 --> 00:47:19.730
That's what we thought
from the beginning,

00:47:19.730 --> 00:47:23.910
that QC programming tends to
have lower regulatory scrutiny.

00:47:23.910 --> 00:47:25.440
It's not typically
submitted along

00:47:25.440 --> 00:47:27.060
with a submission package.

00:47:27.060 --> 00:47:28.740
So therefore, you can have it--

00:47:28.740 --> 00:47:35.070
you can have QC code developed
during the reporting workflow,

00:47:35.070 --> 00:47:37.830
and in R, as opposed
to SAS, and it's not

00:47:37.830 --> 00:47:39.660
going to be challenged as much.

00:47:39.660 --> 00:47:41.220
And we're still
producing everything

00:47:41.220 --> 00:47:42.810
using SAS at the moment.

00:47:42.810 --> 00:47:46.980
So we're taking things that
are produced in a language

00:47:46.980 --> 00:47:49.890
and in a workflow that has
been commonly accepted,

00:47:49.890 --> 00:47:52.320
and we're simply using
our validation processes

00:47:52.320 --> 00:47:55.650
using R. And as
mentioned, that's

00:47:55.650 --> 00:47:58.470
going to facilitate the sort of
on the job organic upscaling,

00:47:58.470 --> 00:48:03.480
because we know that,
if I need to validate

00:48:03.480 --> 00:48:05.310
a summary of
demographics, I can go,

00:48:05.310 --> 00:48:09.150
and I can use R within
our RStudio environment

00:48:09.150 --> 00:48:14.150
and do that work there, as
opposed to launching up SAS.

00:48:14.150 --> 00:48:17.060
Those first few parts, parts
one and two on displays,

00:48:17.060 --> 00:48:19.550
and now moving into part
four, all proof of concepts--

00:48:19.550 --> 00:48:22.400
we're really thinking about
demonstrating the capability

00:48:22.400 --> 00:48:24.867
of using R for stage 2 QC.

00:48:24.867 --> 00:48:27.450
In GSK, that's what we refer to
as independent validation-- is

00:48:27.450 --> 00:48:32.300
stage 2 QC, and back to that
facilitating the on the job

00:48:32.300 --> 00:48:35.060
use of R for upscaling.

00:48:35.060 --> 00:48:39.380
We had a number of
successes with that.

00:48:39.380 --> 00:48:43.510
We had to figure out how to
actually compare data frames.

00:48:43.510 --> 00:48:47.900
We can use PROC COMPARE in SAS,
but how do you do that in R?

00:48:47.900 --> 00:48:49.320
Well, there's a
couple of packages

00:48:49.320 --> 00:48:52.460
that we identified that
could satisfy this,

00:48:52.460 --> 00:48:55.230
and we landed on
the diffdf package.

00:48:55.230 --> 00:48:56.750
And that has been
proven successful

00:48:56.750 --> 00:49:00.380
through our experience so
far to leverage that package.

00:49:00.380 --> 00:49:05.540
We have successful
cross-functional collaboration,

00:49:05.540 --> 00:49:07.497
most specifically
with Andy's group,

00:49:07.497 --> 00:49:09.080
but that was needed,
because we didn't

00:49:09.080 --> 00:49:11.900
have the sort of
internal skillset

00:49:11.900 --> 00:49:14.180
that we needed and knowledge.

00:49:14.180 --> 00:49:16.475
So we needed to work
cross-functionally.

00:49:16.475 --> 00:49:17.930
I mentioned the central tools.

00:49:17.930 --> 00:49:22.010
It also gave us a better vision
of our roadmap towards R,

00:49:22.010 --> 00:49:24.170
and we developed a
significant amount

00:49:24.170 --> 00:49:27.350
of supporting documentation
to help our staff.

00:49:27.350 --> 00:49:29.540
Challenges that we faced
and, I would argue, met.

00:49:29.540 --> 00:49:31.430
One is onboarding
into the-- what

00:49:31.430 --> 00:49:32.940
I would call the R ecosystem.

00:49:32.940 --> 00:49:35.750
So that's the work environment
that's working with Git

00:49:35.750 --> 00:49:39.590
and GitHub, that's
thinking about having to--

00:49:39.590 --> 00:49:41.840
as a programmer, having
to worry about loading

00:49:41.840 --> 00:49:45.470
packages, and installing
packages, and things like that.

00:49:45.470 --> 00:49:47.420
It's just different
than SAS, and so how

00:49:47.420 --> 00:49:51.750
do you take folks that have
been using SAS for 3, 5, 10, 15,

00:49:51.750 --> 00:49:54.830
20 years, and say, well, now
we want you to do this in R.

00:49:54.830 --> 00:49:58.520
How do you help lower the entry
cost for that so that uptake

00:49:58.520 --> 00:50:01.190
can be higher, adoption
can be stronger?

00:50:01.190 --> 00:50:03.770
PROC COMPARE and
diffdf-- diffdf seems

00:50:03.770 --> 00:50:06.410
to be developed
and designed to be

00:50:06.410 --> 00:50:09.520
very similar to PROC COMPARE,
but it has a different look.

00:50:09.520 --> 00:50:10.520
It has a different feel.

00:50:10.520 --> 00:50:14.030
And so we had to work through
those challenges of how you use

00:50:14.030 --> 00:50:17.100
it as a diagnostic tool, not
just an everything compare

00:50:17.100 --> 00:50:20.780
sort of result. Good program
practice in R is different.

00:50:20.780 --> 00:50:22.730
Also try to institute
some sort of code

00:50:22.730 --> 00:50:24.770
review culture, which is--

00:50:24.770 --> 00:50:27.470
I don't know what it's
like in every organization,

00:50:27.470 --> 00:50:30.680
but it's not as strong as
we would like within GSK.

00:50:30.680 --> 00:50:32.420
I think within
clinical reporting--

00:50:32.420 --> 00:50:36.230
I think that's primarily driven
by the independence of QC

00:50:36.230 --> 00:50:42.590
culture, but trying to build
up a code review mentality

00:50:42.590 --> 00:50:44.130
and good program practice.

00:50:44.130 --> 00:50:46.130
And we also notice--
because we're comparing SAS

00:50:46.130 --> 00:50:47.900
to R results, we also
notice that there

00:50:47.900 --> 00:50:50.773
are differences between SAS and
R in some fundamental things.

00:50:50.773 --> 00:50:52.190
Not that either
is right or wrong.

00:50:52.190 --> 00:50:55.870
When we noticed the differences
in the compare output,

00:50:55.870 --> 00:50:58.440
and we dug into it, we realized
that SAS was doing what

00:50:58.440 --> 00:50:59.690
SAS said they should be doing.

00:50:59.690 --> 00:51:02.000
And R was doing what R
said they should be doing,

00:51:02.000 --> 00:51:04.890
and so how do we reconcile that
from a reporting standpoint,

00:51:04.890 --> 00:51:06.350
from an analysis standpoint?

00:51:06.350 --> 00:51:10.700
If our general concept is, well,
things should match exactly,

00:51:10.700 --> 00:51:12.710
and we're not getting
things matching exactly,

00:51:12.710 --> 00:51:13.980
how do we sift through that?

00:51:13.980 --> 00:51:15.920
More importantly, how
do we support our teams

00:51:15.920 --> 00:51:17.030
in navigating that?

00:51:17.030 --> 00:51:20.750
When you open the door to let
loose 250 programmers, 350

00:51:20.750 --> 00:51:23.500
programmers to do this, they
need to know, all right,

00:51:23.500 --> 00:51:25.200
I now see this difference.

00:51:25.200 --> 00:51:27.720
What do I do about
this difference?

00:51:27.720 --> 00:51:30.530
So we started working
through those sorts of things

00:51:30.530 --> 00:51:32.630
with the SAS versus
R differences

00:51:32.630 --> 00:51:34.850
that we were encountering.

00:51:34.850 --> 00:51:36.590
That brought us to part three.

00:51:36.590 --> 00:51:39.860
Part three is
where we go to live

00:51:39.860 --> 00:51:44.600
stage two or independent
QC using R instead of SAS.

00:51:44.600 --> 00:51:47.240
You could think of running it
as like a pilot study, where

00:51:47.240 --> 00:51:50.900
you have a study that says
we're going to just validate

00:51:50.900 --> 00:51:53.930
everything using R.
Instead of that model,

00:51:53.930 --> 00:51:58.900
we chose to have an organic sort
of grassroots effort to doing

00:51:58.900 --> 00:51:59.400
this.

00:51:59.400 --> 00:52:01.970
So any individual programmer
at the output level

00:52:01.970 --> 00:52:05.270
can say, all right, I've been
assigned 25 different displays,

00:52:05.270 --> 00:52:07.370
so I'm going to
choose these three,

00:52:07.370 --> 00:52:10.010
and I'm going to validate
these using R instead of SAS.

00:52:10.010 --> 00:52:12.090
That's the opportunity to use--

00:52:12.090 --> 00:52:14.650
to on the job learn
R and take what

00:52:14.650 --> 00:52:17.150
you've learned from the training
coming out of Andy's group,

00:52:17.150 --> 00:52:21.228
and use it for the work
that you typically do.

00:52:21.228 --> 00:52:23.270
Part of the requirements
of that were making sure

00:52:23.270 --> 00:52:25.850
that, of course, the
leadership on the study

00:52:25.850 --> 00:52:28.393
was aware of what was happening,
because it doesn't put us--

00:52:28.393 --> 00:52:30.560
we don't think it puts a
significant amount of risk,

00:52:30.560 --> 00:52:32.690
but it's something from a
resourcing and timelines

00:52:32.690 --> 00:52:35.300
perspective leadership
needs to know about.

00:52:35.300 --> 00:52:37.730
We have mechanisms in place
to help with ways of working

00:52:37.730 --> 00:52:40.380
and also provide visibility
on what's happening,

00:52:40.380 --> 00:52:43.070
but really it's-- it was
putting in place a model

00:52:43.070 --> 00:52:44.360
of encouragement from above.

00:52:44.360 --> 00:52:46.780
So we had leadership in the
team saying, you can do this.

00:52:46.780 --> 00:52:47.780
We allow you to do this.

00:52:47.780 --> 00:52:50.330
We know it's going to take you
a little bit longer than you

00:52:50.330 --> 00:52:53.000
might feel to do it in SAS,
because you're so fluent in SAS

00:52:53.000 --> 00:52:55.190
and just learning
R. But we're going

00:52:55.190 --> 00:52:57.320
to provide all the support
from below in terms

00:52:57.320 --> 00:52:59.030
of documentation,
and the support team

00:52:59.030 --> 00:53:01.993
to answer your questions,
and things like that.

00:53:01.993 --> 00:53:04.160
On the right here, I'm not
going to go through them.

00:53:04.160 --> 00:53:07.160
It's just we outlined a
number of required components

00:53:07.160 --> 00:53:09.500
before we would say
it's a green light.

00:53:09.500 --> 00:53:12.710
People can go out,
and do this, and use

00:53:12.710 --> 00:53:15.290
R. It is a regulated
environment,

00:53:15.290 --> 00:53:17.780
so we have to be
conservative in this approach

00:53:17.780 --> 00:53:21.140
while we, as well as the
rest of the industry,

00:53:21.140 --> 00:53:24.950
is navigating towards the
ultimate objective of sort

00:53:24.950 --> 00:53:28.670
of freely being able to
submit packages produced

00:53:28.670 --> 00:53:33.198
and using R, and perhaps
other open source languages.

00:53:33.198 --> 00:53:35.240
Some of the support
mechanisms we have in place--

00:53:35.240 --> 00:53:36.380
I'll run through this
quickly, because I

00:53:36.380 --> 00:53:38.860
know that we're running a
little bit short on time.

00:53:38.860 --> 00:53:40.650
We put together a
quick start guide

00:53:40.650 --> 00:53:44.690
to just be a one stop
shop that links out

00:53:44.690 --> 00:53:47.690
to a number of
internal documentation.

00:53:47.690 --> 00:53:50.720
And we put together--
on the left here is

00:53:50.720 --> 00:53:53.720
a guidance document
that's pretty thorough.

00:53:53.720 --> 00:53:58.100
It goes through not just
how to get started, but also

00:53:58.100 --> 00:54:00.170
what does it mean to
develop code in RStudio?

00:54:00.170 --> 00:54:01.970
How do you execute code?

00:54:01.970 --> 00:54:03.950
Some mini vignettes
of going through some

00:54:03.950 --> 00:54:07.080
of our standard
display templates,

00:54:07.080 --> 00:54:11.810
standard summary of AE template
that's very GSK specific.

00:54:11.810 --> 00:54:15.110
But it gives people
sample code, gives things

00:54:15.110 --> 00:54:17.900
that they can copy, and
paste, and slightly tweak.

00:54:17.900 --> 00:54:21.080
When I was learning
SAS along the way,

00:54:21.080 --> 00:54:24.980
I learned by someone
pointing me to a similar type

00:54:24.980 --> 00:54:27.842
output in a different study,
and I would copy that code in.

00:54:27.842 --> 00:54:29.800
And I would start to
manipulate it to make sure

00:54:29.800 --> 00:54:32.570
that it worked for my purposes.

00:54:32.570 --> 00:54:35.860
And so I wasn't starting from a
blank sheet of paper every time

00:54:35.860 --> 00:54:36.578
I started code.

00:54:36.578 --> 00:54:37.870
So that's what we wanted to do.

00:54:37.870 --> 00:54:42.280
This document on the left, which
is using the Bookdown package,

00:54:42.280 --> 00:54:42.810
was--

00:54:42.810 --> 00:54:46.810
it was completely designed
to try and again lower

00:54:46.810 --> 00:54:49.720
that transition cost of
someone moving from SAS into R

00:54:49.720 --> 00:54:53.590
in order to improve uptake
and improve adoption.

00:54:53.590 --> 00:54:55.920
On the right here
is a workplace page.

00:54:55.920 --> 00:54:58.200
When GSK uses workplace,
it's a workplace page

00:54:58.200 --> 00:55:00.730
that we created also
to support staff

00:55:00.730 --> 00:55:02.730
in asking questions,
and communicating out,

00:55:02.730 --> 00:55:04.070
and that sort of thing.

00:55:04.070 --> 00:55:07.300
So one of the interesting
characteristics of this

00:55:07.300 --> 00:55:09.460
is, as we released
this documentation,

00:55:09.460 --> 00:55:14.080
we also got some notice
that there's more

00:55:14.080 --> 00:55:21.280
that R can do than just our
statistical analysis type work

00:55:21.280 --> 00:55:24.310
that you can publish to
this sort of documentation

00:55:24.310 --> 00:55:27.320
to RStudio Connect.

00:55:27.320 --> 00:55:31.030
That's just another reason to
integrate R into the workflows

00:55:31.030 --> 00:55:33.040
that we have.

00:55:33.040 --> 00:55:36.160
So we move into the
R adoption journey.

00:55:36.160 --> 00:55:39.560
Motivations behind this--
to have this expanded tool

00:55:39.560 --> 00:55:42.610
set, to expand our delivery
capabilities, upscaling things

00:55:42.610 --> 00:55:44.600
that we've sort of
already talked about.

00:55:44.600 --> 00:55:47.230
But we do want to become
amongst those industry leaders,

00:55:47.230 --> 00:55:48.370
and not be a follower.

00:55:48.370 --> 00:55:50.440
That's where I think
Andy's group wants

00:55:50.440 --> 00:55:51.573
to position ourselves.

00:55:51.573 --> 00:55:53.740
That's where we want to
position ourselves, as well.

00:55:53.740 --> 00:55:56.350
That's why we have this
sort of outreach stream,

00:55:56.350 --> 00:55:58.410
that sort of thing.

00:55:58.410 --> 00:56:00.510
Coming out of that
project, deliverables.

00:56:00.510 --> 00:56:03.240
We want R-ready study teams
for our conventional delivery.

00:56:03.240 --> 00:56:05.190
We want an R package
development team for--

00:56:05.190 --> 00:56:06.450
to enhance our process--

00:56:06.450 --> 00:56:07.885
for process improvement.

00:56:07.885 --> 00:56:09.510
We want to have shiny
development teams

00:56:09.510 --> 00:56:11.370
for future delivery products.

00:56:11.370 --> 00:56:13.660
And, of course, to have a
robust support strategy.

00:56:13.660 --> 00:56:15.890
And again, we
always have this eye

00:56:15.890 --> 00:56:19.400
on how do we get to
R for submission.

00:56:19.400 --> 00:56:22.460
So going back to the
sort of six or seven work

00:56:22.460 --> 00:56:27.420
streams here in the R adoption
project, the real question

00:56:27.420 --> 00:56:28.920
we have is, how do
you manage change

00:56:28.920 --> 00:56:31.140
within a massive organization
without disrupting

00:56:31.140 --> 00:56:32.640
that delivery capability?

00:56:32.640 --> 00:56:35.550
And in order to do
that, we need to have

00:56:35.550 --> 00:56:36.800
a multifaceted approach.

00:56:36.800 --> 00:56:38.550
That's where these
work streams came from.

00:56:38.550 --> 00:56:40.830
To have focused efforts
within the work stream,

00:56:40.830 --> 00:56:42.810
we had leads in place
for each of these work

00:56:42.810 --> 00:56:46.110
streams that both define the
objectives and the strategy

00:56:46.110 --> 00:56:48.150
towards delivering
to those objectives,

00:56:48.150 --> 00:56:51.390
but those efforts are also
integrated and coordinated

00:56:51.390 --> 00:56:53.340
because this team
works together.

00:56:53.340 --> 00:56:55.620
And the people that are
on each individual team

00:56:55.620 --> 00:56:59.625
and leading those teams
are trying to coordinate

00:56:59.625 --> 00:57:05.490
and with the ultimate objective
of serving the R adoption

00:57:05.490 --> 00:57:08.300
objectives.

00:57:08.300 --> 00:57:10.575
So in the last minute and
a half or two minutes,

00:57:10.575 --> 00:57:12.450
I just want to do a
little bit of a deep dive

00:57:12.450 --> 00:57:16.380
into the essential tools
that some of the packages

00:57:16.380 --> 00:57:19.650
that Andy's team
has created for--

00:57:19.650 --> 00:57:22.870
to serve our teams in the
clinical reporting process.

00:57:22.870 --> 00:57:25.950
So we have two
RStudio add-ins first.

00:57:25.950 --> 00:57:29.410
We have a GSK header add-in
and a GSK log add in.

00:57:29.410 --> 00:57:31.590
The GSK header just inserts--
it's through RStudio.

00:57:31.590 --> 00:57:34.620
It's an interactive way
of inserting and updating

00:57:34.620 --> 00:57:36.380
the header in our R programs.

00:57:36.380 --> 00:57:39.780
GSK log generates an
execution session log.

00:57:39.780 --> 00:57:42.240
There's another add-in, which
is the fourth bullet, called

00:57:42.240 --> 00:57:43.800
copy4qc.

00:57:43.800 --> 00:57:46.320
This is an add-in
that was generated--

00:57:46.320 --> 00:57:48.390
since we're developing
in the warp environment,

00:57:48.390 --> 00:57:50.130
but we need the code--

00:57:50.130 --> 00:57:52.660
the QC code to be
in our study area

00:57:52.660 --> 00:57:54.300
so it can be archived
with the study,

00:57:54.300 --> 00:57:56.670
we have to be able
to get it from one

00:57:56.670 --> 00:58:00.690
system to the other while
maintaining last modified

00:58:00.690 --> 00:58:03.360
dates, while maintaining
the appropriate permissions

00:58:03.360 --> 00:58:05.680
for the systems
that they live in.

00:58:05.680 --> 00:58:08.280
But we also need 350
programmers to be

00:58:08.280 --> 00:58:12.070
able to do this in a
very consistent manner.

00:58:12.070 --> 00:58:13.560
And so the best
way to do that is

00:58:13.560 --> 00:58:16.380
to provide them a tool
where they hit buttons,

00:58:16.380 --> 00:58:18.240
and they have less
and less control--

00:58:18.240 --> 00:58:20.125
limited control over
how that's happening,

00:58:20.125 --> 00:58:22.120
so we can control that process.

00:58:22.120 --> 00:58:23.650
And then we have
two building blocks

00:58:23.650 --> 00:58:25.180
of actually doing the work.

00:58:25.180 --> 00:58:27.840
So GSK table building blocks
with basic summary displays,

00:58:27.840 --> 00:58:33.120
and GSK data set building blocks
of updated set transformations.

00:58:33.120 --> 00:58:35.190
I want to focus a little
bit on the GSK log,

00:58:35.190 --> 00:58:38.310
and that'll close me out,
because this is something

00:58:38.310 --> 00:58:41.760
that we realized early on
was a unique thing here,

00:58:41.760 --> 00:58:45.710
because SAS naturally
generates an execution log.

00:58:45.710 --> 00:58:47.190
R does not do that.

00:58:47.190 --> 00:58:49.230
SAS hits an error,
it keeps going.

00:58:49.230 --> 00:58:51.210
R hits an error, it stops.

00:58:51.210 --> 00:58:53.670
But people are used
to seeing logs,

00:58:53.670 --> 00:58:56.400
and there's a usefulness of
having some sort of execution

00:58:56.400 --> 00:58:59.250
log, because it generates a
record of information that's

00:58:59.250 --> 00:59:00.900
related to that execution.

00:59:00.900 --> 00:59:05.340
So it gets us towards that sort
of GxP compliance requirements

00:59:05.340 --> 00:59:06.660
that we have.

00:59:06.660 --> 00:59:08.520
The rationale-- again,
R doesn't actually

00:59:08.520 --> 00:59:10.950
generate this
information, so we want

00:59:10.950 --> 00:59:12.690
to be able to provide
minimum information

00:59:12.690 --> 00:59:15.240
to demonstrate the
integrity of that execution,

00:59:15.240 --> 00:59:18.402
and details to facilitate
reproduction so

00:59:18.402 --> 00:59:19.860
that, if two years
from now someone

00:59:19.860 --> 00:59:21.060
were to go back, and
look at the program,

00:59:21.060 --> 00:59:22.830
and want to show
reproducibility,

00:59:22.830 --> 00:59:25.020
they could recover what
the R environment looked

00:59:25.020 --> 00:59:27.690
like at the time, and should
be able to reproduce it.

00:59:27.690 --> 00:59:29.310
Maybe not with the
click of a button,

00:59:29.310 --> 00:59:30.893
but with a little
bit of effort, you'd

00:59:30.893 --> 00:59:33.420
have the information to
reset that environment.

00:59:33.420 --> 00:59:35.340
And this is a
challenge or a problem

00:59:35.340 --> 00:59:38.610
that's specific to the
clinical reporting workflow.

00:59:38.610 --> 00:59:41.160
What Andy's group came up
with that we helped advise on

00:59:41.160 --> 00:59:45.260
was not intended to
replicate that SAS log.

00:59:45.260 --> 00:59:47.350
The design was as
an RStudio add-in.

00:59:47.350 --> 00:59:49.150
We have elements of
who, what, and when.

00:59:49.150 --> 00:59:50.230
So who ran the file?

00:59:50.230 --> 00:59:51.160
What was the file?

00:59:51.160 --> 00:59:52.310
When did it start?

00:59:52.310 --> 00:59:54.900
When did it stop?

00:59:54.900 --> 00:59:58.865
We wanted to drop in any
sort of errors and warnings.

00:59:58.865 --> 01:00:00.240
So in this case,
here's a warning

01:00:00.240 --> 01:00:04.170
that the diffdf doesn't have
all values compared equal,

01:00:04.170 --> 01:00:06.840
so you can see that.

01:00:06.840 --> 01:00:09.420
Information about the R
session and information

01:00:09.420 --> 01:00:11.278
about the packages and
versions available.

01:00:11.278 --> 01:00:12.820
We took the path of
least resistance,

01:00:12.820 --> 01:00:14.340
so it's really all
the packages that

01:00:14.340 --> 01:00:18.550
are installed in that
RStudio instance,

01:00:18.550 --> 01:00:20.790
not just the packages
that were used

01:00:20.790 --> 01:00:22.380
for the production
of that program.

01:00:22.380 --> 01:00:27.180
But we do have a sort of
mini backlog of enhancements

01:00:27.180 --> 01:00:31.560
that we want to make
to this package,

01:00:31.560 --> 01:00:35.300
including moving away from
being an add-in and more

01:00:35.300 --> 01:00:39.530
of batch execution
type implementation.

